\chapter{Worked Examples}
\label{ch:examples}

Three complete webs, each showing a different strength: splitting one
program across files, substituting a value inline, and driving several
languages from a single narrative.

\section{One program, several files}

A tiny calculator split into a header and two source files, all from one
web. The header fragment is defined once and referenced from both the
header file and the implementation, so the declaration can never drift
from its use:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
@@d add prototype @@{int add(int a, int b);@@}

@@o calc.h @@{
#ifndef CALC_H
#define CALC_H
@@<add prototype@@>
#endif
@@}

@@o calc.c @@{
#include "calc.h"
@@<add prototype@@>
{ return a + b; }
@@}

@@o main.c @@{
#include <stdio.h>
#include "calc.h"
int main(void) { printf("%d\n", add(2, 3)); return 0; }
@@}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent \verb|weft calc.weft| writes \verb|calc.h|, \verb|calc.c|, and
\verb|main.c|; \hbox{\tt @@<add prototype@@>} expands to the same
declaration in both the header (as a prototype) and the implementation
(as the function head). Change the signature once and all three files
follow.

\section{Substituting a value inline}

A fragment referenced \emph{mid-line} in a scrap is spliced in place,
with no surrounding markers or line breaks --- so a fragment can act as a
single reusable \emph{value}. This is how a version number, a port, or a
host name lives in exactly one place:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
@@d port @@{4000@@}
@@d host @@{"localhost"@@}

@@o config.c @@{
const char *host = @@<host@@>;
int port = @@<port@@>;
@@}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent tangles to a clean, compilable line each:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
const char *host = "localhost";
int port = 4000;
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent Because the references sit after visible text on the line, weft
suppresses the usual section markers and \verb|#line| directives and
splices the value seamlessly (see Chapter~\ref{ch:languages}). Define the
value once; every \verb|@@<port@@>| picks it up.

\section{One concept across languages}

A web is not limited to one language. A single narrative can define a
database schema, the code that uses it, and the script that deploys it,
routing each to its own file:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
@@o schema.sql @@{
CREATE TABLE users (id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, email TEXT NOT NULL);
@@}

@@o app.py @@{
import psycopg2
def find_user(conn, uid):
    cur = conn.cursor()
    cur.execute("SELECT email FROM users WHERE id = %s", (uid,))
    return cur.fetchone()
@@}

@@o deploy.sh @@{
#!/bin/sh
psql "$DATABASE_URL" -f schema.sql
@@}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent One \verb|weft| run tangles all three files, each with the
right comment style for its language in the section markers, while the
prose explains the \emph{one} idea --- the users table --- that ties them
together. The cross-language contract is documented exactly where the
languages meet.
